        tags: ["Project Auth"], operationId: "projectAuthAdminSetPasswordProtection", summary: "Replace the password protection of this project environment",
        description: "Owner or administrator only with trusted same-origin validation. Since 2.53.0. The body names all three values at once; a body that names only the switch would leave open what should happen to the minimum length and the wording. The check itself is offline: no password and no prefix of its hash ever leaves this installation, and no third party such as Have I Been Pwned is contacted. It is enforced in the service at the two places where a password is set, at sign-up and at a password reset, never in the console and never in this route, which never receives a password at all. A refused password is a 400; with the wording named the answer says that the password appears in a known credential leak, with generic only that it does not meet the policy of the project. Neither answer says how often it occurs or which leak it came from. Unlike the rate limit counter this check does not fail open. A deployment points QKERN_PROJECT_AUTH_LEAKED_PASSWORD_FILE at a file of hex SHA-1 or SHA-256 digests or prefixes, at most one million entries and 16 MiB, all of the same length; a missing or malformed file keeps Project Auth from starting instead of falling back silently. Without a file a built-in list of 25 entries applies, the ranks 1 to 25 of the yearly published SplashData list Worst Passwords of the Year 2019; all 25 are shorter than the twelve characters the service demands anyway, so without a file the check refuses nothing the length rule does not already refuse. Every change writes the audit entry project_auth.password_protection.changed with the switch, the minimum length, the wording and the origin and size of the list; a refused password writes project_auth.password.refused with the reason and no password, no digest and no address.",
